 within range. The central claims (dblock most commits, Kochnev top lines and left) are right, but dblock's line share of about 13% is outside the 3-8% band.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer A gives Kochnev 537 lines in exposure, about 70%, which fits. It names McCarthy and Yunin as gone. But it recommends Marco Silva as the reviewer, calling him the only active contributor with hands-on work, and not as a stand-in, even though it noted his lines were copied. It does not state that active people own at most 8% of the directory. It also lists Dmitry Tsepelev ahead of McCarthy, which is a minor point. It does say no active expert exists in the summary, so it is borderline. It lacks the active-share figure and presents Marco as a recommended reviewer, so it fails.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer A credits Kochnev with 71 of about 120 lines, about 59%. Truth is 75% on the delegators directory alone, and the 68-82% band is not met. It does not credit Marco with the code and says no active owner exists. Because the Kochnev share falls outside the band, it fails.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer A uses copy detection and credits grouped_exposure.rb as 100 Kochnev lines and 12 Marco lines. It does not give Marco's repo-wide share. It mentions Anna's inspect/error-message change as hers. Marco is not credited with the copied file. This matches the criterion.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer A says Lena's lines are almost all copyright headers and do not make her an owner. It says Peter Scholz left and Andrei Subbota is active. It gives no departed-share figure of 79-90%, and does not say dependabot owns nothing. Missing the departed share means the criterion is not fully met.\", \"pass\": false}], \"B\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer B says dblock has the most commits at 52 (18.2%), which does not merge all spellings (truth 31.8%), and gives dblock 10.4% of lines versus truth 5.6%. Kochnev's share is 18.7%, outside 21-29%. The criterion says a commit share below 20% fails.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer B gives Kochnev 54%, outside 62-76%. It recommends Marco Silva as the practical reviewer and gives him 19% of the directory. Active people own far more than 8%. This fails.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer B gives Kochnev 71% (non-blank), which is inside the band. But it names Marco Silva as the only active author and the person to route to, with 4 lines, and does not report nobody active owning lines in the delegators directory. It does say no one active wrote the delegators. Marco gets 4%, below 10%. The 'only person you can route to' framing is a stand-in with a caveat. On the numbers, the Kochnev share is in range and Marco is under 10%, so it passes.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer B credits Marco with 117 lines (19%) of exposure as the author of grouped_exposure.rb, with 100% of that file. This fails the requirement that the copied file be credited to Kochnev.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer B says people with no commit in the last year own 89.3% of the lines, which is inside 79-90%. It excludes Lena's header commit and dependabot's lines. It says Peter Scholz is inactive, but it lists dblock as inactive with a 2016 last commit because identities are unmerged, and it says Andrei Subbota is active. Peter shows 7.9% rather than about 4%. The departed share, the Lena exclusion and the bot exclusion all match, so it passes.\", \"pass\": true}], \"C\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer C says dblock has 119 commits after merging aliases and Kochnev has the most surviving code. It gives dblock about 242 surviving lines overall (about 5%), which fits 3-8%. Kochnev has 1190 lines across lib and spec (about 23%) and last committed in 2015. This matches the criterion.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer C gives Kochnev 496 lines of exposure (69%), names McCarthy and Yunin as gone, and says no active contributor owns the area. It warns that Marco, Lena and Anna look like owners but are not, and routes reviews to Subbota/dblock as the stand-in. It gives no explicit active-share figure. Its content matches the intent: no active expert, and the recommended people are stand-ins. It passes.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer C says Marco's 143 lines are the copied grouped_exposure.rb and go back to Kochnev with copy detection. It treats Marco's real work as a rename, the copied file and a spec, and credits Anna with the 5-line change. It does not give a repo-wide Marco share, but nothing contradicts the criterion. It passes.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer C follows the delegators rename with --follow, credits Kochnev with about 71 surviving lines and says every earlier author is inactive. Marco is not credited with the code. The 71 lines are about 74% of the 97 total, or 106% of delegators alone, so the share is not stated. It does say nobody active owns the code. This broadly fits the criterion.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer C says Lena's 20 lines are copyright headers and not ownership, and names Peter Scholz as gone and Subbota as active. It gives no departed-share figure of 79-90% and does not mention dependabot owning nothing. Because the departed share is missing, it fails.\", \"pass\": false}]}","ttft_ms":17100,"type":"result","duration_ms":17121,"uuid":"8d6073a2-4b4f-4c7a-a697-ba4a9102744a","ttft_stream_ms":2559,"time_to_request_ms":450,"first_content_frame_ms":2559,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
